===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|de|Robot}}, from a West Slavonic language, ultimately related to Etymology 2, below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|now|historical}} A system of [[serfdom]] used in Central Europe, under which a tenant's rent was paid in forced labour. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1849|title=Littell's Living Age|volume=23|page=309
|passage=“I say again, down with the '''''robot'''''!—he is a dog who yields it!”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author={{w|Tim Blanning}}|title=The Pursuit of Glory|page=159|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2008
|passage=Although the '''''robot''''' varied from region to region, it was rarely less than burdensome.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃erbʰ-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|cs|robot}}, from {{m|cs|robota||drudgery, servitude}}. Coined in the 1920 science-fiction play ''{{w|R.U.R.|R.U.R. (Rossum's Universal Robots)}}'' by {{w|Karel Čapek}} after having been suggested to him by his brother {{w|Josef Čapek|Josef}}, and taken into English without change.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://blog.archive.org/2021/03/24/major-scifi-discovery-hiding-in-plain-sight-at-the-internet-archive/ |title=Major SciFi Discovery Hiding in Plain Sight at the Internet Archive |last=Adams |first=Caralee |date=2021-03-24 |publisher={{w|Internet Archive}} |work=Internet Archive Blogs |lang=en}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
[[Image:KUKA robot for flat glas handling.jpg|thumb|Industrial '''robot''' handling flat glass.]]

# {{lb|en|chiefly|science fiction}} An [[intelligent]] [[mechanical]] [[being]] [[design]]ed to look like a [[human]] or other [[creature]], and usually made from [[metal]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1923|translator=Paul Selver|author=Karel Čapek|authorlink=Karel Čapek|title={{w|R.U.R.}}|page=13|passage=Domain: In fact, he rejected man and made the '''Robot'''. My dear Miss Glory, the '''Robots''' are not people. Mechanically they are more perfect than we are, they have an enormously developed intelligence, but they have no soul.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=26 January 2010|author=Tom Chivers and Iain McDiarmid|work=The Telegraph|passage=The '''robots''' in Dick's novel, loosely adapted by Ridley Scott into the film Blade Runner, were so similar to humans that when they went rogue, trained bounty hunters were called in to perform psychological tests to see whether suspected androids lacked human empathy.}}
# A [[machine]] built to carry out some [[complex]] [[task]] or group of tasks by physically [[move|moving]], especially one which can be [[programmed]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|We have a '''robot''' in the house that does the vacuuming.}}
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:robot}}
#: {{hyper|en|automaton}}
#: {{hypo|en|android}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=16 May 2010|author=Tim Webb|work=The Guardian|passage=It's painfully slow and complex work which has never been attempted before in these conditions: the small box-shaped '''robots''', equipped with two claws, are operating in almost freezing water 5,000ft below the surface, in pitch black and strong currents.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[person]] who does not seem to have any [[emotions]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Murray N. Rothbard|title=Making Economic Sense|page=xiv|passage=Yet surely he was a humorless '''robot''' of a man, spewing forth lonely and bitter critiques of all those lesser mortals with whom he could not identify.|year=2006}}
# {{lb|en|South Africa}} A [[traffic light]] (from earlier {{m|en|robot policeman}}). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{lb|en|surveying}} A [[theodolite]] which follows the movements of a [[prism]] and can be used by a one-man [[crew]].
# {{lb|en|dance}} A style of [[dance]] popular in [[disco]] in which the dancer [[imitate]]s the stiff movements of a stereotypical [[android]] robot.

